\chapter{Logistic regression II: checking and extending}

A fitted logistic model raises two separate questions, and people constantly
confuse them:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Discrimination.} Can the model tell the cases from the non-cases?
        Measured by the ROC curve and the area under it (AUC).
  \item \textbf{Calibration.} When the model says 30\%, does it happen about 30\%
        of the time? Measured by calibration plots and goodness-of-fit tests.
\end{enumerate}

A model can discriminate beautifully and be badly calibrated, or be perfectly
calibrated and useless at discriminating. You need both.

<<ch4-setup, echo=FALSE>>=
data(birthwt, package = "MASS")
bw <- within(birthwt, {
  race  <- factor(race, levels = 1:3, labels = c("white", "black", "other"))
  smoke <- factor(smoke, levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
  ht    <- factor(ht,    levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
  ui    <- factor(ui,    levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
})
fit1 <- glm(low ~ age + lwt + race + smoke + ht + ui, family = binomial, data = bw)
@

\section{Turning probabilities into decisions}

<<confusion>>=
phat <- fitted(fit1)                  # predicted probabilities
pred <- ifelse(phat > 0.5, 1, 0)      # classify at the usual 0.5 cut-off
tab  <- table(predicted = pred, observed = bw$low)
tab
@

<<confusion-stats>>=
sens <- tab["1", "1"] / sum(tab[, "1"])     # sensitivity: caught cases / all cases
spec <- tab["0", "0"] / sum(tab[, "0"])     # specificity: caught controls / all controls
acc  <- sum(diag(tab)) / sum(tab)
c(sensitivity = sens, specificity = spec, accuracy = acc)
mean(bw$low == 0)                            # accuracy of "always predict 0"
@

\begin{warningbox}[Accuracy is a trap]
Here the model's accuracy is \Sexpr{round(acc, 3)}, and simply predicting ``never
low birth weight'' for everybody scores \Sexpr{round(mean(bw$low == 0), 3)}. When
one class is more common, accuracy mostly measures how common that class is.
Never report accuracy alone.
\end{warningbox}

The 0.5 cut-off is a convention, not a law. If missing a case is much worse than a
false alarm, lower it.

\section{The ROC curve and AUC}

The ROC curve draws sensitivity against $1 -$ specificity as you slide the cut-off
from 1 down to 0. Rather than reach for a package, here is the whole thing in nine
lines --- it demystifies the plot completely.

<<roc-fn>>=
roc_manual <- function(prob, y) {
  ord <- order(prob, decreasing = TRUE)
  y   <- y[ord]
  tpr <- cumsum(y)      / sum(y)         # true positive rate at each cut-off
  fpr <- cumsum(1 - y)  / sum(1 - y)     # false positive rate
  auc <- sum(diff(c(0, fpr)) * (c(0, head(tpr, -1)) + tpr) / 2)   # trapezoid rule
  list(fpr = c(0, fpr), tpr = c(0, tpr), auc = auc)
}
r <- roc_manual(phat, bw$low)
r$auc
@

<<roc-plot, fig.width=9, fig.height=4.2, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: the ROC curve. The diagonal is a coin flip; the further towards the top-left corner, the better. Right: how sensitivity and specificity trade off as the cut-off moves.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
plot(r$fpr, r$tpr, type = "l", lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"],
     xlab = "1 - specificity (false positive rate)",
     ylab = "sensitivity (true positive rate)",
     main = sprintf("ROC curve, AUC = %.3f", r$auc))
grid_book()
polygon(c(r$fpr, 1), c(r$tpr, 0), col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.10), border = NA)
abline(0, 1, lty = 2, col = PAL["grey"], lwd = 2)

cuts <- seq(0.01, 0.99, by = 0.01)
se <- sapply(cuts, function(k) mean(phat[bw$low == 1] >= k))
sp <- sapply(cuts, function(k) mean(phat[bw$low == 0] <  k))
plot(cuts, se, type = "l", lwd = 3, col = PAL["red"], ylim = c(0, 1),
     xlab = "probability cut-off", ylab = "rate", main = "The trade-off")
grid_book()
lines(cuts, sp, lwd = 3, col = PAL["green"])
abline(v = cuts[which.max(se + sp)], lty = 3, col = PAL["accent"], lwd = 2)
legend("right", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = 3,
       col = c(PAL["red"], PAL["green"], PAL["accent"]), lty = c(1,1,3),
       legend = c("sensitivity", "specificity", "best sens+spec"))
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What AUC actually measures]
Pick one case and one non-case at random. The AUC is the probability that the
model gives the case a higher score. AUC $= 0.5$ is a coin flip; $1.0$ is perfect.
Roughly: 0.6 poor, 0.7 acceptable, 0.8 good, 0.9 excellent --- but the right
threshold depends entirely on what the model is for.
\end{plainenglish}

\begin{warningbox}[This AUC is optimistic]
We computed the AUC on the same data used to fit the model. That always flatters
it. For an honest figure use cross-validation or a held-out sample.
\end{warningbox}

<<cv-auc>>=
set.seed(7)
K     <- 10
folds <- sample(rep(1:K, length.out = nrow(bw)))
oof   <- numeric(nrow(bw))                     # out-of-fold predictions
for (k in 1:K) {
  m_k <- glm(formula(fit1), family = binomial, data = bw[folds != k, ])
  oof[folds == k] <- predict(m_k, newdata = bw[folds == k, ], type = "response")
}
c(in_sample = r$auc, cross_validated = roc_manual(oof, bw$low)$auc)
@

\section{Calibration}

Sort the predictions into bins, and compare the average prediction in each bin with
the proportion actually observed. On a perfectly calibrated model the points sit on
the diagonal.

<<calibration, fig.width=6.6, fig.height=4.2, out.width="0.75\\textwidth", fig.cap="Calibration plot with 10 equal-sized bins. Points near the diagonal mean the predicted probabilities can be taken at face value.">>=
par_book()
g   <- cut(phat, breaks = quantile(phat, probs = seq(0, 1, 0.1)), include.lowest = TRUE)
obs <- tapply(bw$low, g, mean)
exp_ <- tapply(phat,  g, mean)
n_g <- tapply(bw$low, g, length)
se_g <- sqrt(obs * (1 - obs) / n_g)
plot(exp_, obs, pch = 19, col = PAL["primary"], cex = 1.4, xlim = c(0, 0.8),
     ylim = c(0, 0.9), xlab = "mean predicted probability",
     ylab = "observed proportion", main = "Calibration in 10 bins")
grid_book(); abline(0, 1, lwd = 2, lty = 2, col = PAL["grey"])
arrows(exp_, obs - 1.96*se_g, exp_, obs + 1.96*se_g,
       length = 0.03, angle = 90, code = 3, col = PAL["primary"])
lines(lowess(exp_, obs), col = PAL["accent"], lwd = 2.5)
@

\subsection{The Hosmer--Lemeshow test}

The same idea turned into a formal test: bin the predictions into $g$ groups
(usually 10), then compare observed and expected counts with a chi-squared
statistic on $g-2$ degrees of freedom.

<<hl-test>>=
hoslem <- function(y, phat, g = 10) {
  cuts <- quantile(phat, probs = seq(0, 1, length.out = g + 1))
  grp  <- cut(phat, breaks = cuts, include.lowest = TRUE)
  obs1 <- tapply(y, grp, sum);              obs0 <- tapply(1 - y, grp, sum)
  exp1 <- tapply(phat, grp, sum);           exp0 <- tapply(1 - phat, grp, sum)
  X2   <- sum((obs1 - exp1)^2 / exp1) + sum((obs0 - exp0)^2 / exp0)
  data.frame(X2 = X2, df = g - 2, p.value = pchisq(X2, g - 2, lower.tail = FALSE))
}
hoslem(bw$low, phat)
@

\begin{warningbox}[How to read a goodness-of-fit $p$-value]
Here a \emph{large} $p$-value is the reassuring one: no evidence of poor fit. But
this test is weak. It has little power in small samples, it is sensitive to the
arbitrary number of bins, and in very large samples it rejects fit that is
practically fine. Use it as a sanity check alongside the calibration plot, never
as the only evidence.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Residuals and influence}

Raw residuals $y - \hat p$ are almost useless for a binary outcome --- they can
only take two values for any given $\hat p$, so a residual plot looks like two
curved stripes. Two better versions exist.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pearson residual:} $r_i = \dfrac{y_i - \hat p_i}{\sqrt{\hat p_i(1-\hat p_i)}}$,
        which standardises by the expected variability.
  \item \textbf{Deviance residual:} the signed square root of each observation's
        contribution to the deviance. This is \R's default from \fn{residuals()}.
\end{itemize}

<<binom-resid, fig.width=9, fig.height=4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: the two-stripe pattern that makes raw residual plots useless for binary data; the smoother is what you actually read. Right: influence, via Cook's distance.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
plot(fitted(fit1), residuals(fit1, type = "deviance"),
     pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.4), col = "white", cex = 1.1,
     xlab = "fitted probability", ylab = "deviance residual",
     main = "Deviance residuals (read the smoother)")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, col = PAL["grey"], lwd = 1.5)
lines(lowess(fitted(fit1), residuals(fit1, type = "deviance")),
      col = PAL["red"], lwd = 3)

cd <- cooks.distance(fit1)
plot(cd, type = "h", lwd = 2, col = PAL["primary"], xlab = "observation",
     ylab = "Cook's distance", main = "Influence")
grid_book()
abline(h = 4 / nrow(bw), lty = 2, col = PAL["red"])
text(which(cd > 4/nrow(bw)), cd[cd > 4/nrow(bw)],
     labels = which(cd > 4/nrow(bw)), pos = 3, cex = 0.65, col = PAL["red"])
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What you are looking for]
In the left panel, the red smoother should hug zero. A systematic bend means the
model has the wrong shape --- perhaps a predictor should be curved, or an
interaction is missing. In the right panel, tall spikes are observations that
would move the coefficients noticeably if deleted. Investigate them; do not delete
them without a reason.
\end{plainenglish}

A binned residual plot is often more readable than either:

<<binned-resid, fig.width=6.6, fig.height=4, out.width="0.75\\textwidth", fig.cap="Binned residual plot. Average the residuals within bins of fitted value; roughly 95\\% of the points should fall inside the grey band if the model fits.">>=
par_book()
br <- function(x, r, nbins = 15) {
  b  <- cut(x, breaks = quantile(x, seq(0, 1, length.out = nbins + 1)),
            include.lowest = TRUE)
  data.frame(x = tapply(x, b, mean), y = tapply(r, b, mean),
             se = tapply(r, b, function(z) 2 * sd(z) / sqrt(length(z))))
}
bb <- br(fitted(fit1), residuals(fit1, type = "response"))
plot(bb$x, bb$y, pch = 19, col = PAL["primary"], cex = 1.3,
     ylim = range(c(bb$y - bb$se, bb$y + bb$se, 0)) * 1.2,
     xlab = "mean fitted probability in bin", ylab = "mean residual in bin",
     main = "Binned residuals")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, col = PAL["grey"], lwd = 1.5)
lines(bb$x, bb$se, lty = 2, col = PAL["red"]); lines(bb$x, -bb$se, lty = 2, col = PAL["red"])
@

\section{Grouped (binomial) data}

Often your data arrive already aggregated: ``out of $n$ animals in this herd, $k$
got the disease''. That is still logistic regression --- the outcome is just
binomial rather than Bernoulli.

<<cbpp-fit>>=
data(cbpp, package = "lme4")
head(cbpp, 4)
## Two-column response: successes and failures
mb <- glm(cbind(incidence, size - incidence) ~ period, family = binomial, data = cbpp)
summary(mb)
@

\begin{keyidea}[Three ways to give \R{} a binomial response]
\fn{glm(cbind(k, n-k) \textasciitilde{} x, binomial)} --- successes and failures. \\
\fn{glm(prop \textasciitilde{} x, binomial, weights = n)} --- proportion plus a weight. \\
\fn{glm(y \textasciitilde{} x, binomial)} with \fn{y} a 0/1 vector or a two-level factor. \\
All three fit the same model. The first two let you detect overdispersion; the
third cannot.
\end{keyidea}

\section{Overdispersion in binomial models}

For grouped data, the binomial says $\Var(k) = np(1-p)$ exactly. Real data often
vary more, because animals within the same herd are not independent. Check the same
way as for counts:

<<binom-overdisp>>=
c(residual_deviance = deviance(mb), df = df.residual(mb),
  ratio = deviance(mb) / df.residual(mb))
## The Pearson version is the one to trust
X2 <- sum(residuals(mb, type = "pearson")^2)
c(pearson_X2 = X2, df = df.residual(mb), dispersion = X2 / df.residual(mb))
pchisq(X2, df.residual(mb), lower.tail = FALSE)     # p-value for "no overdispersion"
@

A dispersion of \Sexpr{round(X2/df.residual(mb), 2)} means the data are about
\Sexpr{round(X2/df.residual(mb), 1)} times more variable than the binomial allows.
Standard errors from that model are too small by roughly
$\sqrt{\Sexpr{round(X2/df.residual(mb), 2)}} = \Sexpr{round(sqrt(X2/df.residual(mb)), 2)}$.

The quick repair is a quasi-binomial fit, which multiplies all the standard errors
by that square root:

<<quasibinom>>=
mq <- glm(cbind(incidence, size - incidence) ~ period,
          family = quasibinomial, data = cbpp)
summary(mq)$dispersion
round(cbind(binomial = coef(summary(mb))[, "Std. Error"],
            quasibinomial = coef(summary(mq))[, "Std. Error"]), 4)
@

The coefficients are identical; only the standard errors change. The proper repair
--- modelling \emph{why} herds differ --- is a mixed model, which we do in
Chapter 15 with this same dataset.

\begin{warningbox}[You cannot detect overdispersion in ungrouped 0/1 data]
With a Bernoulli outcome the residual deviance carries no information about
dispersion, so the deviance/df ratio is meaningless. If your data are truly one row
per individual with no grouping, ``binary overdispersion'' is not a thing you can
test for --- what you can have instead is correlation between individuals, which
needs a random effect or a robust standard error.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Complete separation}

If some combination of predictors perfectly predicts the outcome, the maximum
likelihood estimate runs off to infinity. \R{} warns you --- but the warning is
easy to miss.

<<separation, warning=TRUE, message=TRUE>>=
sep <- data.frame(x = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), y = c(0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1))
ms  <- glm(y ~ x, family = binomial, data = sep)
round(coef(summary(ms)), 3)
@

Notice the absurd estimate with an enormous standard error, and a $p$-value near 1
despite a perfect relationship. Signs of separation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item coefficients in the tens or hundreds;
  \item standard errors in the thousands;
  \item the warning \fn{fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1 occurred};
  \item residual deviance essentially zero.
\end{itemize}

Fixes, in order of preference: combine sparse categories; drop the offending
predictor; or use penalised likelihood (Firth logistic regression, in the
\pkg{logistf} or \pkg{brglm2} packages), which always gives finite estimates.

\section{Other links for binary data}

The logit is not the only choice.

<<links-compare>>=
m_logit  <- glm(low ~ lwt + smoke, family = binomial(link = "logit"),    data = bw)
m_probit <- glm(low ~ lwt + smoke, family = binomial(link = "probit"),   data = bw)
m_cll    <- glm(low ~ lwt + smoke, family = binomial(link = "cloglog"),  data = bw)
round(rbind(logit = coef(m_logit), probit = coef(m_probit), cloglog = coef(m_cll)), 3)
sapply(list(logit = m_logit, probit = m_probit, cloglog = m_cll), AIC)
@

<<links-plot, fig.width=6.8, fig.height=3.8, out.width="0.78\\textwidth", fig.cap="Three link functions on the same data. Logit and probit are nearly indistinguishable; the complementary log-log is asymmetric.">>=
par_book()
nd <- data.frame(lwt = seq(80, 250, 2), smoke = "yes")
plot(nd$lwt, predict(m_logit, nd, type = "response"), type = "l", lwd = 3,
     col = PAL["primary"], ylim = c(0, 0.8), xlab = "mother's weight (lbs)",
     ylab = "P(low birth weight)", main = "logit vs probit vs cloglog")
grid_book()
lines(nd$lwt, predict(m_probit, nd, type = "response"), lwd = 3, lty = 2, col = PAL["accent"])
lines(nd$lwt, predict(m_cll,    nd, type = "response"), lwd = 3, lty = 3, col = PAL["green"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", lwd = 3, lty = 1:3, cex = 0.85,
       col = c(PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"], PAL["green"]),
       legend = c("logit", "probit", "cloglog"))
@

\begin{rulebox}
Use the logit unless you have a specific reason not to. It is the only one whose
coefficients exponentiate to odds ratios, which is why every applied field reports
it. The probit gives practically identical fitted probabilities. The cloglog is
genuinely different and is the right choice when the binary outcome comes from an
underlying Poisson process (``did at least one event happen?''), which links it
naturally to survival analysis.
\end{rulebox}
